    "phrase": "Boundaries & exchange",
    "seed": 1294289988,
    "framing": "The synthesis / Editorial interpretation What connects this week The common thread is equilibrium response. Environmental spending changes demand and public budgets. Carbon policy changes fossil-fuel prices across borders. A climate tipping point changes the relative productivity and amenity value of places. Storage changes the very price spreads that finance storage. Corporate tax rules change firms’ cost of capital differently depending on how they finance investment. That makes incidence , counterfactual reasoning , and complementarity central. The first-order policy lever is rarely the final outcome. Prices move, firms and households substitute, capital reallocates, and people migrate. Those responses can reinforce the intended effect, dilute it, or move costs somewhere else. For research, the challenge is to keep model-based counterfactuals, observational estimates, and identified causal effects distinct. A long-run spatial simulation is not an event-study estimate. A cross-country fiscal shock is not a randomized experiment. A structural electricity model can reveal mechanisms that a reduced-form average misses, but only conditional on its market assumptions. This week’s research question: when a policy changes one price, which margin moves next—and does that response undo or amplify the intended effect?",
