    "date": "2026-10-03",
    "no": "008",
    "identity": "Field Brief",
    "theme": "complements",
    "framing": "Today’s developments span a cooling U.S. labor market, record European heat exposure, enforcement at two very different financial and technological borders, and another contraction at a major Triangle research institution. They do not form a single trend; together they show where official rules and institutional capacity are being tested by slower growth, physical risk and evasive networks. The developments are distinct; the common constraint is capacity. The labor report concerns demand and monetary policy; the European heat record concerns physical exposure; the two enforcement actions concern evasion through financial and commercial networks; and RTI’s cuts concern regional research capacity. None should be collapsed into a single causal story. The practical connection is narrower: public rules work only when institutions can observe, enforce and adapt. Better data clarify labor and heat risks, while export controls, sanctions and research systems depend on staffing, verification and cross-border cooperation.",
    "version": 3,
    "subject": "capacity",
    "composition": "capacity-observation",
    "signature": "issue:capacity-observation",
    "phrase": "Observe, enforce & adapt",
